+// Round 13 rework -- why dragging STILL did not move any window
+// on device in v14, and what changed. Two independent defects,
+// each sufficient on its own, both invisible to the desktop host
+// harness the earlier rounds relied on:
+//
+//   (1) The drag could only ARM on an event delivery the iOS
+//       path never guarantees. The press branch required
+//       windowOf(watched) == m_host, but the first -- and only
+//       guaranteed -- delivery of a touch on this platform is
+//       the QTouchEvent addressed to the top-level QWindow
+//       (a QWidgetWindow), for which windowOf() returns nullptr
+//       (it is a QWindow, not a QWidget). Arming therefore
+//       depended on a later widget-level re-delivery of a
+//       touch-synthesized QMouseEvent, and on this Qt iOS kit
+//       synthesized mouse events for a finger drag are not
+//       reliably produced at all (the FEBio Studio iOS port on
+//       the same Qt 6.9.3 kit hit the identical failure class
+//       and had to handle QTouchEvent directly). The press
+//       branch now arms purely by COORDINATES: the bar exists
+//       only as a child of m_host, so a press inside the bar's
+//       global rectangle is a press on the host's drag handle
+//       no matter which object Qt addressed the event to, and
+//       the QTouchEvent itself (TouchBegin/TouchUpdate, both
+//       delivered to the application filter at window level)
+//       drives the whole drag with no synthesis anywhere in
+//       the chain. A dedicated "Move" grip at the bar's left
+//       end gives the handle a finger-sized target: the old
+//       bar was ~94% button by area, so a real finger aiming
+//       for its "background" landed on Minimize or Close.
+//   (2) A maximized or full-screen window silently discards
+//       every geometry change on the iOS QPA. QIOSWindow::
+//       setGeometry() stores the rect as the window's normal
+//       geometry but, while windowState() != Qt::WindowNoState,
+//       does NOT apply it to the view; it re-issues the current
+//       state and forces a layout that reports the ACTUAL
+//       (maximized) geometry back. KiCad's editor frames are
+//       maximized by default (EDA_BASE_FRAME::LoadWindowState
+//       calls Maximize() when the stored state is default and
+//       m_maximizeByDefault is set, as the schematic, PCB,
+//       gerber and page-layout editors all set it), so every
+//       drag Move() -- and every minimize-animation step --
+//       was thrown away by the platform layer. Desktop Qt does
+//       the opposite (a setGeometry there CLEARS the maximized
+//       state), which is exactly why the host harness passed
+//       both failed rounds. The drag now leaves the
+//       maximized/full-screen state at the moment it is
+//       recognised (and minimizeCurrent() does the same before
+//       docking): showNormal() first, an explicit floating
+//       geometry second, and only then delta moves, all while
+//       the window is in Qt::WindowNoState, the one state in
+//       which QIOSWindow::applyGeometry() sets the view frame.
+//
+// TouchCancel is handled too: the QPA sends it when a system
+// gesture or interruption steals the touch, and the old code
+// left m_pressArmed set, so a later unrelated move event could
+// drag a window nobody was holding.
+//
 // The implementation compiles only for iOS (Q_OS_IOS); other wxQt
 // builds get the empty stub at the bottom.
 // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
